At RAN #86 in December 2019 a work item for NTN was agreed (RP-193234,[1]). The normative activities include development of specifications for transparent payload-based LEO. In this document we discuss aspects related to DL-UL timing relations for NTN operation of NR. During RAN1#104-e this topic was discussed, and the following agreements and decisions were reached: Agreement: Update of K_offset after initial access is supported Agreement: Confirm the following working assumption: K_offset can be applied to indicate the first transmission opportunity of PUSCH in Configured Grant Type 2 in the same way as K_offset is applied to the transmission timing of DCI scheduled PUSCH. Agreement: Update of K_offset after initial access is supported Agreement: For unpaired spectrum, extend the value range of K1 from (0..15) to (0..31) FFS: Whether there is an impact on the size of the PDSCH-to-HARQ_feedback timing indicator field in DCI.

{"chunk_id":"R1-2602796-008","collection":"ran1_tdoc_chunks","metadata":{"source":["ETRI"],"tdocNumber":"R1-2602796","title":"Discussion on HARQ related Aspects"},"point_id":"9799931725841922201"}
n Topic#5 [3], cross-layer HARQ and ARQ integration, including NACK-to-ACK error detection/reporting, was discussed but not agreed as a RAN1 study item. Meanwhile, RAN2 has progressed on this topic with the following agreements. In RAN2#131b, the study item on ARQ based on tight coordination with HARQ was agreed [4]. In RAN2#132, the high-level study direction for fast ARQ mechanism was agreed as “start with UL first, then consider DL later” and “ARQ mechanism based on HARQ process status” [5]. In RAN2#133, the following aspects are identified as RAN1 design decisions [6]: UL HARQ-ARQ: signalling of “HARQ process failure” DL HARQ-ARQ: improvement of HARQ feedback reliability (e.g. to mitigate HARQ NACK-to-ACK error) Beyond the above two RAN1 aspects explicitly identified in RAN2 agreements, PHY-layer combining for ARQ retransmission could also be considered as a RAN1 design aspect. In NR, soft combining is exclusively managed within the HARQ framework at the physical layer—the PHY layer controls soft buffer allocation, LLR storage, and combining operations based on HARQ process ID, NDI, and RV. When ARQ retransmission occurs, the physical layer currently treats it as an independent initial transmission because no HARQ-related context is preserved across the ARQ boundary. Enabling soft combining for ARQ retransmissions therefore requires PHY-layer design changes: specifically, how the physical layer identifies that an ARQ retransmission corresponds to a previous transmission, how soft buffer entries are retained or mapped across HARQ-ARQ boundaries, and how combining is performed when the retransmitted data may differ in segmentation or size. These are inherently physical layer design aspects that fall within RAN1 scope. Proposal 11: Study the following for RAN1 aspects of cross-layer HARQ and ARQ interaction signalling of “HARQ process failure” (e.g. the explicit HARQ process failure indication in DCI) improvement of HARQ feedback reliability (e.g. to mitigate HARQ NACK-to-ACK error) PHY-layer combining for ARQ (e.g. to improve reliability of ARQ by preserving soft combining)

